<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hemis Monastery</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hemis Monastery is the largest and wealthiest Buddhist monastery in Ladakh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, about 40 km south-east of Leh. It belongs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drukpa-kagyu">Drukpa lineage</a> and was founded in the 17th century under the patronage of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengge-namgyal">Sengge Namgyal</a>. Its summer festival, the Hemis Tsechu, honours <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmasambhava">Padmasambhava</a> with masked dances, and every twelve years a huge thangka is displayed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hemis-monastery.jpg" alt="Hemis Monastery" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon Canon EOS REBEL T4i · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hemis_Monastery_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
